    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Concrete floor paint almost always peels for one of three reasons: moisture pushing up through the slab, a surface that was not clean or rough enough for the paint to grip, or the wrong product or conditions, such as hot tires on a one-part garage paint, incompatible coats or coats rolled on too thick. You can usually tell which by where the paint is failing and by what is stuck to the back of a peeled flake. Moisture failures cluster at low spots, perimeters and cracks and often show white powder or dampness underneath; prep failures peel in clean sheets with smooth backs; hot-tire failures sit in the wheel paths. The fix is always the same in outline: find the cause, remove all the loose paint, correct the surface, then recoat or switch to a finish that suits the slab.
        </p>

        <div id="causes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Causes Concrete Floor Paint to Peel?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Paint stays on concrete only as long as its bond to the surface is stronger than whatever is trying to push or pull it off. Peeling means that balance tipped. Either the bond was weak from the start, because the paint landed on dust, oil, old sealer or a weak cement skin, or something strong attacked a reasonable bond, such as water vapor from the ground or a hot tire on a soft film. Most failures involve a bit of both.
          </p>

          <p>
            The table below matches what you are seeing to the usual cause. Use it as a starting point, then read the matching section for how to confirm it. It is common to find two causes on one floor, for example a garage with peeling in the tire paths and blistering along the back wall where the slab sits against soil.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What you see</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Most likely cause</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">First thing to check</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Blisters, sometimes with water or white powder inside</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moisture vapor or efflorescence from below</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Plastic sheet test</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Peeling at perimeter, low spots or along cracks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moisture from below</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Grading, gutters, drainage</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Large sheets lifting with smooth, clean backs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Paint never bonded: sealer, smooth trowel finish, no etch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water-drop test on bare spot</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Flakes with a thin layer of concrete on the back</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Weak surface layer (laitance) or dust</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Scratch the bare concrete</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dark or greasy blotches, peeling in patches</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Oil or grease contamination</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Location of old drips</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Peeling only in the tire paths</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hot-tire pickup on a soft film</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Paint type and cure time before parking</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Top coat peeling off an intact bottom coat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Incompatible coats or missed recoat window</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">What was under the new paint</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            One reassurance: peeling is rarely random, and it is rarely the fault of a single bad can of paint. Floor paints from major brands are well tested on properly prepared concrete. When a floor fails, the explanation is almost always in the slab or the process, which is good news, because both are things you can correct before the next coat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="moisture" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Is Moisture Pushing the Paint Off the Concrete?
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the most common cause on basement floors and older garage slabs. Concrete is porous, and a slab poured on the ground can pass water vapor upward continuously, especially if it was poured without a vapor barrier underneath, as many older slabs were. Vapor that reaches a paint film collects under it. Pressure builds, the bond weakens, and the film blisters and eventually lets go. Water-based floor paints are somewhat breathable, but a thick film still slows vapor enough to cause trouble.
          </p>

          <p>
            Liquid water is a harsher version of the same problem. When the water table rises or water collects against the foundation, hydrostatic pressure can push liquid water up through cracks and pores. Floors with this problem often show damp patches after heavy rain, peeling along cracks and at the joint where floor meets wall. Our guide to <a href="/water-coming-through-basement-floor/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">water coming through a basement floor</a> explains how to tell seepage from condensation and what the real fixes are.
          </p>

          <p>
            Efflorescence is the white, chalky clue. As moisture moves through concrete it carries dissolved salts, and when the water evaporates at the surface the salts are left behind as white crystals. Under paint, those crystals collect between the film and the concrete, break the bond and push flakes off. If you find white powder on the back of a flake or on the bare concrete beneath it, moisture is involved. Our guide to <a href="/efflorescence-on-basement-walls/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">efflorescence</a> covers the chemistry and cleanup.
          </p>

          <p>
            Seasons matter. Moisture-related peeling often worsens in spring or after a wet stretch, when the soil is saturated, and it may appear to stop in a dry summer. That is why a floor can look perfect for six months and then fail. A dry-season moisture test can be misleading, so if you suspect moisture, test again in a wet month before repainting.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE TELLTALE SIGN</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Damp or powdery underneath means moisture.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Peel a loose flake. If the concrete beneath feels cool and damp, darkens when exposed, or has a white crystalline residue, repainting without solving the moisture will produce the same result.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Was the Concrete Prepared Properly?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Prep failures are the second big category, and they are the most avoidable. Paint needs clean, sound, slightly rough concrete. Four things commonly get in the way: an old sealer or curing compound, oil and grease, a smooth steel-troweled surface with no texture, and a weak, powdery top layer of cement known as laitance. Each one produces a different kind of failure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sealers and curing compounds are invisible. Many new slabs are sprayed with a curing compound when poured, and many older floors have had a clear sealer applied at some point. Paint rolled over them may look fine at first but cannot reach the concrete, so it peels in clean sheets. The water-drop test catches this: on a bare, scraped spot, water should soak in and darken the concrete within a minute or so. If it beads, something is sealing the surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Oil and grease are common on garage floors. Paint will not bond to them, and residual oil often wicks back up through the film as dark spots before the paint lifts. Patches of peeling where the car's engine sat, or where a lawn mower or generator was stored, point to contamination that a quick rinse did not remove. A smooth, dense trowel finish is another quiet culprit; without etching or grinding, paint has too little texture to grip.
          </p>

          <p>
            Laitance is a thin layer of weak cement paste and fine particles that can form on top of a slab when it is finished. Paint bonds to it well enough, but the laitance itself is weak and lets go from the strong concrete below. If the back of a peeled flake carries a thin, gritty layer of concrete, the bond held and the surface underneath failed. Grinding removes laitance; etching helps but does not always fully remove it. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-prepare-concrete-for-epoxy/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">preparing concrete for a coating</a> covers the methods that work.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tires" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Why Is the Paint Peeling Only Where the Car Tires Sit?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Peeling confined to the wheel paths is almost always hot-tire pickup. After a drive, tires are warm and pliable, and they press into the floor coating with the weight of the car. Chemicals in tire rubber can soften some coatings. As the tire cools overnight, it contracts and grips the surface. When you pull out, the tire can pull the paint off with it, leaving bare patches in the shape of the tread.
          </p>

          <p>
            One-part floor paints, including the epoxy-acrylic garage floor paints, are more vulnerable to this than true two-part epoxy or polyaspartic coatings. The risk is highest in the first weeks, before the film has fully hardened. Driving on the paint before the label time, commonly about seven days for garage floor paints, is the usual trigger. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-does-concrete-paint-take-to-dry/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long concrete paint takes to dry</a> explains why the vehicle wait is so much longer than the walk-on time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prep plays a part too. A paint film with a strong bond to properly profiled concrete resists hot-tire pickup much better than one sitting on a smooth or contaminated surface. If paint lifted in the tire paths after a long cure, look at the back of the flakes: a clean, smooth back points to weak adhesion that the tires simply exposed. Our dedicated guide to <a href="/hot-tire-pickup-epoxy-garage-floor/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">hot tire pickup</a> goes deeper on the mechanism.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you park daily and keep losing paint in the tire paths, it may be time to accept that paint is the wrong product for that floor. A two-part epoxy system, ideally with a durable topcoat, is designed for vehicle traffic. Our <a href="/garage-floor-paint-vs-epoxy/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">garage floor paint vs epoxy</a> comparison explains the cost and performance gap. If you have already used epoxy and it is peeling, see our separate guide on <a href="/epoxy-garage-floor-peeling/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">epoxy garage floor peeling</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Could It Be the Paint or the Way It Was Applied?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Incompatible coats are a frequent cause of peeling between layers rather than from the concrete. The classic case is water-based paint rolled directly over old oil-based or alkyd floor enamel, which is too slick and too hard for latex to bond to without a primer. Painting over a glossy epoxy without sanding, or over a silicone or wax-based sealer, produces the same result. If the top coat peels while the layer underneath stays put, look at what was under it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coats that are too thick also fail. A heavy coat skins over on top while the paint underneath stays soft and full of water. It cures slowly and unevenly, shrinks as it does, and can wrinkle, crack or lose adhesion. Floor paint is designed to be applied at a specific spread rate, commonly around 300 to 400 square feet per gallon on smooth concrete. A gallon that covered half that area was probably applied too heavily.
          </p>

          <p>
            Conditions during painting matter as well. Water-based paint applied below about 50°F may not coalesce into a proper film. Paint applied on a slab in direct sun or above about 90°F can skin over and trap moisture. Condensation on a cool slab, often in a humid basement or a garage on a warm morning after a cold night, puts a thin layer of water under the paint before it even dries. Labels typically require the surface to be at least 5°F above the dew point for this reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timing errors round out the list. Painting new concrete before it is about a month old traps excess water. Recoating too soon softens the first coat; recoating after a product's maximum recoat window without scuffing leaves the second coat with nothing to grab. And the wrong product, such as interior wall paint or a general masonry paint on a floor, can fail simply because it was never meant to handle foot traffic, scrubbing and alkalinity.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="diagnose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Do You Diagnose Why Your Floor Paint Is Peeling?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the pattern. Map where the paint is failing. Perimeter walls, low spots, cracks and the area nearest the downhill side of the house point to moisture. Wheel paths point to hot tires. A spot where a car or machine has been dripping points to oil. Large areas peeling evenly across the floor point to a prep problem such as an old sealer or no etching. Peeling between coats, with sound paint underneath, points to compatibility or recoat timing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, read the flakes. Peel a loose piece and look at its back. Smooth and clean means the paint never bonded to the concrete. A thin coat of gritty concrete stuck to it means the surface layer was weak. White crystals mean efflorescence. Dampness on the flake or on the exposed concrete means active moisture. A smooth layer of a different color or sheen means the coat underneath was the problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then test the slab. On a bare area, tape down a square of clear plastic, about 18 by 18 inches, sealed on all four edges, for 16 to 24 hours; condensation or darkened concrete under it confirms moisture. Our <a href="/epoxy-garage-floor-moisture-test/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete moisture test guide</a> covers this and the more precise calcium chloride and in-slab humidity tests. On another bare area, drip water: beading means a sealer is still present. Scratch the bare concrete with a key; if it powders easily, the surface is weak.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, check the sound paint. Press a strip of strong tape onto paint that still looks fine and rip it off. If it comes up easily, the whole floor is at risk and patching will not hold; the problem is everywhere, just not visible yet. If the sound paint holds firmly, the failure is local, and spot repairs are realistic. This one test usually decides whether you are facing a touch-up or a strip-and-redo.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fix" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Do You Fix Peeling Concrete Floor Paint?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fix the cause first, or the repair will fail the same way. For moisture, that means improving drainage, extending downspouts, regrading soil away from the slab, sealing cracks, or in serious cases installing interior drainage. For oil, it means aggressive degreasing. For hot tires, it means a better coating or a longer cure. Recoating over an unsolved problem only buries it under more paint that will peel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, remove everything loose. For small areas, scrape with a stiff floor scraper and feather the edges of the surrounding sound paint with sandpaper so the patch does not show a ridge. For large failures, a pressure washer with a surface cleaner attachment will strip poorly bonded paint quickly, and a chemical stripper or a rented floor grinder removes the rest. If the paint could be from before 1978, test it for lead before sanding or grinding, as EPA guidance advises, and use a grinder with a vacuum shroud and appropriate respiratory protection for silica dust.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then re-prep and recoat properly: degrease, etch or grind to a light profile, let the slab dry, fill cracks, prime where the label calls for it and apply two thin coats in the right conditions. Our step-by-step guide on <a href="/how-to-paint-a-concrete-floor/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how to paint a concrete floor</a> covers each stage. When choosing paint the second time, our <a href="/best-concrete-floor-paint/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete floor paint picks</a> show which products suit basements, porches and garages.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When should you stop painting and switch to stain or sealer?</h3>

          <p>
            If the moisture test still fails after you have done what you reasonably can, stop fighting the slab with a film. A penetrating stain for color plus a breathable penetrating sealer lets vapor escape instead of trapping it, so it cannot blister or peel. Our <a href="/concrete-stain-vs-paint/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete stain vs paint</a> comparison and our <a href="/best-penetrating-concrete-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">penetrating concrete sealer</a> guide cover that route. For a garage with daily parking and good, dry concrete, the opposite upgrade, a two-part epoxy, is usually the answer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting over the peeling areas</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rolling fresh paint over loose or blistered paint glues the new coat to a layer that is already letting go. Within weeks, both layers lift together. Scrape or strip every loose area back to sound paint or bare concrete, feather the edges, and prepare the exposed concrete properly before any new paint goes on.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blaming the paint brand</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It is tempting to switch brands and repeat the same process, but most peeling comes from the slab or the prep, not the can. If the cause was moisture, oil, old sealer or laitance, a new brand applied the same way will fail the same way. Diagnose first, then choose the product.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Testing for moisture only in summer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A plastic sheet test in a dry month can pass on a slab that passes plenty of moisture in spring. If paint failed with signs of moisture, such as blisters, white powder or damp concrete, retest during a wet season or after heavy rain before deciding the slab is dry enough to repaint.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the tape test on sound paint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The paint that still looks fine may be barely attached. A quick tape test across several areas tells you whether you can spot-repair or need to strip the whole floor. Patching small areas on a floor with weak adhesion everywhere leads to a cycle of endless touch-ups that never stop.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Grinding old paint without precautions</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Grinding concrete and old coatings releases crystalline silica dust, and paint from before 1978 may contain lead. Use a grinder with a vacuum shroud and HEPA extractor, wear a properly fitted respirator rated for the hazard, and test old paint for lead first. Dry sweeping the dust spreads it around the house.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I just touch up peeling concrete floor paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if the problem is local and the surrounding paint passes a tape test. Scrape the loose paint, feather the edges, degrease and lightly etch or sand the bare spot, then prime if needed and apply thin coats from the same paint. If the sound paint lifts easily with tape, the whole floor is at risk and touch-ups will keep failing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my basement floor paint bubbling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Bubbles or blisters on a basement floor usually mean moisture vapor is collecting under the paint film. Pop one: water, dampness or white powder inside confirms it. Less often, bubbles come from painting in direct heat or rolling a coat too thick. A plastic sheet test on bare concrete will tell you whether moisture is the cause.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a primer stop concrete paint from peeling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A primer helps when the problem is adhesion to porous, chalky or previously coated concrete, or when switching between oil-based and water-based paint. It does not stop moisture pushing up from below, and it cannot bond through oil, old sealer or laitance. Primer is part of good prep, not a substitute for cleaning, profiling and a dry slab.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I remove peeling paint from a concrete floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Scrape loose paint with a long-handled floor scraper, then use a pressure washer with a surface cleaner to strip poorly bonded areas. For well-bonded remnants, use a chemical stripper rated for the paint type or rent a floor grinder with dust extraction. Test old paint for lead first if it might predate 1978, and wear eye and respiratory protection.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my porch paint peeling on concrete steps?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Outdoor steps take every stress at once: rain, standing water, freeze-thaw cycles, sun and foot traffic. Peeling often starts where water sits or where the step meets the house. Common causes are moisture under the film, poor prep and paint applied in cold or damp weather. A penetrating stain and sealer can be more durable on exposed steps.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can efflorescence come back after I clean it off?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if moisture keeps moving through the concrete. Cleaning removes the existing salt deposits, but as long as water carries dissolved salts to the surface, new crystals will form, including under fresh paint. Reduce the moisture source first, then clean, rinse and let the slab dry. Persistent efflorescence is a strong sign paint is the wrong finish.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is peeling paint on a concrete floor dangerous?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually it is a cosmetic and tripping concern, not a health hazard. The exceptions are old paint that may contain lead, common in homes built before 1978, and the dust created when grinding or sanding concrete. Test suspect paint for lead before disturbing it, and control dust with a vacuum shroud and proper respiratory protection.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

