        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Peeling concrete floor paint is the slab telling you something. Moisture, contamination, a weak or slick surface, hot tires or a bad application each leave a recognizable pattern, and five minutes with a loose flake, a strip of tape and a square of plastic sheeting will usually point to the right one.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Fix that cause before you buy more paint. Strip what is loose, prepare the concrete properly and recoat in good conditions. If the slab simply will not stay dry, switch to a penetrating stain and breathable sealer, and if the floor carries a car every day, step up to a two-part epoxy. More paint on the same problem is the one fix that never works.
          </p>
